```swift
override func viewDidLoad() {
    super.viewDidLoad()
    // one-time setup: subviews, data source wiring
}

override func viewWillAppear(_ animated: Bool) {
    super.viewWillAppear(animated)
    // runs every time before appearing: refresh data, start animations
}

override func viewDidDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {
    super.viewDidDisappear(animated)
    // pause timers, save state
}
```

- **loadView()**: creates the view hierarchy if not using a storyboard/nib; rarely overridden directly.
- **viewDidLoad()**: called once after the view is loaded into memory — the standard place for one-time setup.
- **viewWillAppear / viewDidAppear**: called every time the view is about to become / has become visible — good for refreshing data or starting animations.
- **viewWillDisappear / viewDidDisappear**: called every time the view is about to leave / has left the screen — good for pausing work.

A common interview trap: code in viewDidLoad() runs only once per VC instance, so it's wrong for anything that needs to refresh each time the screen re-appears (e.g. returning from a pushed screen).

ARC automatically inserts retain/release calls at compile time based on static analysis of ownership, so developers don't manually manage reference counts. But ARC cannot break cycles on its own.

```swift
class ViewModel {
    var onUpdate: (() -> Void)?
}

class ViewController {
    let viewModel = ViewModel()

    func setup() {
        // Retain cycle: viewModel -> closure -> self, and self -> viewModel
        viewModel.onUpdate = {
            self.reloadData()
        }
    }

    func setupFixed() {
        viewModel.onUpdate = { [weak self] in
            self?.reloadData()
        }
    }
}
```

Use `weak` when the reference can legitimately become nil (e.g. delegate patterns, closures held by a longer-lived object). Use `unowned` only when you're certain the referenced object will never outlive the one holding the reference — getting this wrong causes a crash rather than a silent nil.

```swift
struct CounterView: View {
    @State private var count = 0

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Count: \(count)")
            Button("Increment") { count += 1 }
        }
    }
}
```

- **@State**: local, view-owned mutable state — SwiftUI manages its storage outside the struct so it survives re-renders.
- **@Binding**: a two-way reference to state owned by a parent, letting a child view read and write it (`Toggle("On", isOn: $isOn)`).
- **@StateObject**: creates and owns a reference-type `ObservableObject`, guaranteed to be created only once even if the view re-initializes.
- **@ObservedObject**: references an `ObservableObject` owned elsewhere (e.g. passed in) — using it to *create* the object risks re-creating it on every re-render, which is the classic interview gotcha versus @StateObject.

```swift
// GCD
DispatchQueue.global().async {
    let data = fetchData()
    DispatchQueue.main.async {
        self.update(with: data)
    }
}

// Structured concurrency
Task {
    let data = await fetchData()
    await MainActor.run { self.update(with: data) }
}
```

GCD works with queues and closures — flexible, but manual about which queue you're on, and error handling means propagating Result types or optional callbacks yourself.

Swift's `async/await`, `Task` and `actor` types provide structured concurrency: a child Task's lifetime is tied to its parent, cancellation propagates automatically, and thrown errors work with ordinary `try/catch`. `@MainActor` replaces manually dispatching back to the main queue for UI updates.
